&lt;p&gt;Join the Columbia Economics Society for an intensive two-day &lt;strong&gt;Financial Modeling, Valuation &amp;amp; Interview Prep Bootcamp&lt;/strong&gt; led by &lt;strong&gt;Ashish Kohli&lt;/strong&gt;, an investment banking professional with over 20 years of transaction experience who has taught this program at Columbia for over a decade.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Designed for students interested in &lt;strong&gt;investment banking, private equity, consulting, and corporate finance&lt;/strong&gt;, this hands-on bootcamp combines practical financial modeling, business valuation, and preparation for finance and consulting interviews.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Throughout the workshop, participants will gain experience building and analyzing financial models, valuing businesses, and understanding how financial concepts are applied in real-world investment banking, private equity, and corporate finance transactions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Additional Career Preparation Benefits&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Interview Preparation Guide:&lt;/strong&gt; All participants will receive an interview preparation guide prepared by the instructor to help them prepare for finance and consulting interviews.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;R&amp;eacute;sum&amp;eacute; Review:&lt;/strong&gt; Participants will have the opportunity to have their r&amp;eacute;sum&amp;eacute;s reviewed by the instructor and the Columbia Economics Society.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Practical Interview Preparation:&lt;/strong&gt; The workshop will strengthen students&amp;#39; understanding of financial concepts, valuation techniques, and analytical problem-solving skills relevant to finance and consulting interviews.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Event Details&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Day 1:&lt;/strong&gt; Sunday, November 8, 2026 | 9:00 AM&amp;ndash;5:30 PM&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Day 2:&lt;/strong&gt; Sunday, November 15, 2026 | 9:00 AM&amp;ndash;5:30 PM&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Location:&lt;/strong&gt; Northwest Corner Building, Room 501, Columbia University&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Registration Fee:&lt;/strong&gt; $199 per participant (includes both days)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructor:&lt;/strong&gt; Ashish Kohli&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Whether you&amp;#39;re preparing for &lt;strong&gt;finance or consulting internships and interviews&lt;/strong&gt;, or simply looking to develop practical financial and analytical skills, this bootcamp offers an opportunity to learn directly from an experienced investment banking professional.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Spaces are limited. Register now to secure your spot!&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;IIJS welcomes Noa Yedlin to discuss her new book, &lt;em&gt;House Arrest,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/em&gt;she is joined in conversation by IIJS Prof. Naama Harel.&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Monday, November 9, at noon | 617 Kent Hall&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Barak Fogel, an unemployed professor, newly separated from his wife, awakes one morning to startling news: his beloved mother, deputy director of the Theodore Hirsch Centre for Sustainable Peace, professor of Israel studies, a refined collector of Israeli art and an avid member of several charitable associations&amp;mdash;is suspected of embezzling 3.4 million shekels from her place of work.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Barak now attempts to navigate within this turbulent reality: between his sister, who is convinced their mother is innocent, and his skeptical brother; between the wife he has just separated from and who is still present in everyone&amp;rsquo;s lives, and his friends who disappear; between old mistakes and new insults &amp;ndash; all played out in front of a relentless Media.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;More than anything, he must reconsider the character of the mother that he knows so well. Who is this woman with the wonderful sense of humour, sharp wit and a ready inclination for righteousness, who now sits under house arrest?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;House Arrest takes apart its characters with a sharpened knife, building from them an original, tumultuous novel about family and money, motherhood and children, failure and success, social standing and self-image.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Noa Yedlin&lt;/strong&gt; is an Israeli author, the recipient of the Sapir Prize (the Israeli Man Booker) and the Prime Minister&amp;#39;s Literature Award and author of the bestselling House Arrest, Stockholm, People Like Us and The Wrong Book. Yedlin was named by Haaretz Magazine one of &amp;quot;66 Israeli Women You Should Know&amp;rdquo;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Yedlin is also the creator of a two-season TV series based on her bestselling novel Stockholm (the Israeli Best Mini-Series TV Award). A German remake of the series (You Don&amp;#39;t Die Among Friends) won Best Scripted Format at 2021 International Format Awards and Best European Series at La Rochelle Festival De La Fiction 2021. The series is now being re-made by SVT Sweden (broadcast: Christmas 2022). Her bestselling novel People Like Us is currently being developed into a series in Israel. A stage adaption of House Arrest is now playing at Beit Lessin Theater in Tel Aviv.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Yedlin is considered one of Israel&amp;#39;s top writers. Her novels &amp;ndash; widely known for their dark and subtle humor - sketch with biting precision the complex relationships both within the family and outside of it, while capturing and deconstructing the spirit of the times. Yedlin deals with global phenomena that infiltrate the personal realm and shape our everyday lives - the rise and fall of social elites, shifts in the balance of power and the never-ending battle between individualism and conformism.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;*Guests must register by Thursday, November 5, to be approved for campus access; unregistered guests will not be permitted on campus. Each guest must register individually using a unique email address.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.eventbrite.com/e/house-arrest-a-book-talk-with-noa-yedlin-tickets-1997165087539?aff=oddtdtcreator" target="_blank"&gt;Click here to register for this event&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;You can attend this event in-person or online.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Join the Committee on Forced Migration for an exciting launch and discussion of two new books:&lt;/strong&gt; Neeraj Kaushal&amp;rsquo;s &amp;quot;When Africa Comes to America&amp;quot; (2026, Columbia University Press) and Bernadette Ludwig&amp;rsquo;s &amp;quot;Unwelcome Shores. Black Refugees in America&amp;quot; (2025, Rutgers University Press).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;quot;When Africa Comes to America&amp;quot; provides an expert analysis of the myths and realities surrounding African immigration today. Economist Neeraj Kaushal argues that in the second half of the 21st century, Africans will account for the largest share of immigrants to the United States. Challenging the view that African emigration is driven by poverty, war, and disaster, she demonstrates that the continent&amp;rsquo;s sizable and growing middle-class has both the aspirations and the means to relocate. First- and second-generation African immigrants are often highly skilled, and the children of Black African immigrants reach higher educational attainment than U.S.-born whites and earn comparable incomes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Based on data from a longitudinal ethnographic study, sociologist Bernadette Ludwig, examines the racialization of Black refugees and the anti-Black racism they have experienced at every step of their migration journey. Focusing on Liberian refugees&amp;rsquo; experiences, &amp;quot;Unwelcome Shores&amp;quot; shows that Black refugees and asylum seekers face specific burdens when it comes to refugee resettlement quotas and requirements, systemic and interpersonal forms of anti-Black racism and dominant narratives in which Black refugees&amp;rsquo; humanity is often ignored in favor of overemphasizing presumed barbaric violence, endemic wars, and cultural backwardness. The book equally explores how Black refugees respond to these exclusions&lt;em&gt;.&lt;/em&gt; In the end, &amp;quot;Unwelcome Shores&amp;quot; underscores how integration is deeply contingent on race, place, and historical context and highlights that anti-Black racism is not limited to native-born Black Americans but also shapes immigration policies and how Black immigrants and refugees are (not) welcomed to the United States.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Authors&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://socialwork.columbia.edu/directory/neeraj-kaushal" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Neeraj Kaushal&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, Professor of Social Work, Columbia School of Social Work&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.bernadettemludwig.com/" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Bernadette Ludwig&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, Sociologist and Assistant Dean, Macricostas School of Arts &amp;amp; Sciences, Western Connecticut State University; Affiliated Faculty with The New School&amp;#39;s Zolberg Institute on Migration &amp;amp; Mobility&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Discussants&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
