&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Registration REQUIRED by 4pm on November 9, 2026 in order to attend this event.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Please join the East Central European Center and the Harriman Institute for a book panel on &lt;em&gt;The Routledge Handbook of 1989 and the Great Transformation&lt;/em&gt;, featuring editors &lt;strong&gt;Rosamund Johnston&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Philipp Ther&lt;/strong&gt;. Moderated by &lt;strong&gt;Małgorzata Mazurek&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This cutting-edge collection of essays analyzes 1989 as a pivotal year for exploring longer-term economic, social, political, and cultural transformations linked to the rise of neoliberalism and globalization since the 1970s and continuing to the present.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Drawing on Karl Polanyi, the handbook argues that the &amp;ldquo;great transformation&amp;rdquo; presented here began well before 1989. It also brings Eastern and Central Europe into conversation with other global regions, while challenging straightforward core-periphery dichotomies. The contributions to this volume provide different case studies&amp;mdash;some national, some comparative, some international or global&amp;mdash;each illustrating particular trends and developments.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The handbook is directed at students and scholars of East Central Europe, of global and economic history, and at scholars of sociology, anthropology, and political science interested in post-1989 transformation, neoliberalism, and globalization.&lt;/p&gt;

