&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;CTLgrads Learning Community - &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The Good, the Bad, and the Ugly: The Role of Feelings and Emotions in the Classroom&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt; (Session 2) - for graduate students&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Teaching and learning have never been purely intellectual processes. Emotions in the classroom can be good, bad, and sometimes ugly, but they are always part of learning. Curiosity motivates students to engage with new ideas, joy fosters a sense of belonging, frustration can signal that a student needs additional support, and anxiety can make participation more difficult. Yet emotions are often treated as separate from, or even disruptive to, academic work. This Learning Community explores the role of feelings and emotions as an integral part of teaching and learning, for students and instructors alike. We will explore how emotional awareness can support engaged, inclusive, and rigorous teaching. Attendees will reflect on the emotions present in their own classrooms, consider how those emotions can facilitate or hinder learning, and develop practical strategies for responding to them intentionally.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;This second session of two-part Learning Community will be held in-person&lt;/strong&gt; and will be co-facilitated by Anna Leigh Garner and Juan Camilo Rojas Gomez, Senior Lead Teaching Fellows at the Center for Teaching and Learning Community. Participants should register for and attend &lt;strong&gt;both&lt;/strong&gt; sessions of this LC.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;In Session 2, &lt;/strong&gt;attendees will turn to the emotions we experience as instructors and TAs: anxiety before a difficult class, frustration when students seem disengaged, or joy when a student has a breakthrough. Our emotions shape our teaching in ways we may not always notice, from the topics we choose for our syllabi to how we respond to students in the moment. Participants will reflect on how recognizing their own emotional responses can help them avoid reacting impulsively and instead make more intentional pedagogical decisions. We will then connect emotional awareness to student-centered, empathetic, and inclusive pedagogy, considering how an emotionally responsive classroom can complement rigorous teaching while maintaining clear academic goals and boundaries. Each participant will leave having identified one or two concrete changes to bring into their own teaching. Finally, we will return to a central question: what might change in our teaching if we understood emotions not as distractions from learning, but as part of how learning happens?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Session 1 will convene on Friday, 11/06, 11:40-12:55pm in room 212 Butler Library; registration is available &lt;a href="https://events.columbia.edu/cal/event/eventView.do?b=de&amp;amp;calPath=%2Fpublic%2Fcals%2FMainCal&amp;amp;guid=CAL-00bbdb70-a0e70b2f-01a0-e95c3f66-000055cfevents@columbia.edu&amp;amp;recurrenceId="&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Session 2 will convene on Friday, 11/13, 11:40-12:55pm in room 212 Butler Library.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Each session of this LC counts as a Pedagogy Workshop for the&lt;a href="https://tdp.ctl.columbia.edu/"&gt; &lt;strong&gt;Teaching Development Program (TDP)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. See the&lt;a href="https://tdp.ctl.columbia.edu/about/faqs/"&gt; &lt;strong&gt;TDP FAQs&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; for more information about obtaining credit for this and other graduate student-led events through the CTL.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Who Gets to Be Neutral? Culture, Credibility, and the Mediator&amp;#39;s Seat&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Classical mediation asks the neutral third party to stand equidistant from both sides. But what happens when the parties themselves aren&amp;#39;t standing on equal ground? In this conversation, we ask whether neutrality is always the right goal, or whether it can quietly preserve the very imbalances mediation claims to address. Join NECR lecturer Dr. Dianne Williams and special guest Dr. Faith Speaks for a conversation that moves past &amp;quot;how do I stay neutral?&amp;quot; toward the harder question: what is a mediator actually accountable to?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Drawing on mediation and restorative-practice frameworks alongside Dr. Speaks&amp;#39; background in counseling psychology and diversity-and inclusion-facilitation, this session explores how personal worldviews can shape who gets read as credible in a room, how a facilitator&amp;#39;s own background is never a blank slate, and how cultural awareness operates beneath the surface of every dispute. Rather than a single-discipline take, this is a genuinely multidisciplinary hour&amp;mdash;psychology and conflict resolution in direct conversation&amp;mdash;designed to give prospective students a real sense of the analytical range the NECR program cultivates.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Speakers:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Dr. Dianne Williams,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;Lecturer, Negotiation and Conflict Resolution; International Mediator and Restorative Practitioner&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;(moderator)&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Dr. Faith Speaks,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;Counseling Psychologist, Personal and Organizational Development Consultant&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Dr. Faith Speaks is a counselor psychologist, researcher, therapist, educator, civil and human rights organizer, and community development professional whose commitment to human dignity, justice, education, and social change began during her youth growing up in Brooklyn and Long Island, New York.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For more than four decades, her work has spanned counseling psychology, human development, education, behavioral health, corrections, re-entry, community development, organizational leadership, and human rights.&lt;/p&gt;

