&lt;p&gt;Join us on &lt;strong&gt;November 13 at 7:00pm in Pupin 301 &lt;/strong&gt;for a talk by grad student Kiyan Tavangar about stellar streams.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Title: &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Galaxies are cannibals &amp;mdash; how can we use that to understand dark matter&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Abstract: &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;em&gt;Galaxies like our Milky Way form over billions of years by absorbing smaller galaxies. In the process, the absorbed galaxies get stretched into structures known as &amp;ldquo;stellar streams&amp;rdquo;, whose properties may hold the key to understanding dark matter.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;rdquo;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We&amp;#39;ll start the evening at &lt;strong&gt;7:00pm &lt;/strong&gt;with some astronomy trivia, followed by the talk and Q&amp;amp;A. From 8:10-9:00pm, we will be outside observing on campus (weather permitting).&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If you do not have a CUID or would like to bring someone who doesn&amp;rsquo;t have one, please follow the instructions and register here: &lt;a href="https://forms.gle/v5BZRHAhphR6v5TcA"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;https://forms.gle/v5BZRHAhphR6v5TcA&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;strong&gt; &lt;/strong&gt;by November 11 midnight.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;How do we bring previously disparate ways of knowing, Indigenous Knowledge and &amp;ldquo;Western&amp;rdquo; or mainstream science, into right relationship with one another for mutual thriving of land and culture? At a time of accelerating environmental change and complex, overlapping challenges, we need a plurality of perspectives to innovate solutions. This talk focuses on work being carried out by a team of international, interdisciplinary, predominantly Indigenous scientists from the US, Canada, Australia, and Aotearoa/New Zealand as part of the newly funded Center for Braiding Indigenous Knowledges and Science. It will share how a team which includes archaeologists, climate, environmental, and water scientists; and scholars with expertise in Indigenous knowledge systems, foodways, science ethics; Indigenous education; museums and heritage are collaborating to explore ethical practices and protocols of braiding knowledges, the seeds for building new research worlds. The talk highlights efforts to bring braiding methodologies into mainstream scientific practice through a transdisciplinary approach that reflects Indigenous understandings of place in which the urgent and interconnected areas of climate change, cultural places, and food sovereignty and security are the focus.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Event Speaker&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.umass.edu/anthropology/about/directory/sonya-atalay" target="_blank"&gt;Sonya Atalay&lt;/a&gt;, Provost Professor of Anthropology at the University of Massachusetts Amherst&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Event Information&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Free and open to the public; &lt;a href="https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSc6Up1VfBvMiwWChCOfdfJhB4fjkSQmR1YMNufc3XpsA8I2Nw/viewform?usp=publish-editor" target="_blank"&gt;registration&lt;/a&gt; required. Registration closes at 1 PM on November 13. Contact &lt;a href="mailto:scienceandsociety@columbia.edu"&gt;scienceandsociety@columbia.edu&lt;/a&gt; with any questions.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Part of the &lt;a href="https://scienceandsociety.columbia.edu/content/co-production-knowledge-initiative"&gt;Co-Production of Knowledge Initiative&lt;/a&gt; at the Center for Science and Society.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Meet our visiting Scholars: &lt;strong&gt;Michael Katagaya&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Digital Suppression and the Right to Political Participation: Exploring a Human Rights Accountability Framework for Democracy and Service Delivery in the Digital Electoral Environment&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Democratic participation now increasingly depends on digital infrastructure &amp;mdash; connectivity, platforms, information environments, and the civil society networks that monitor them. International human rights law has left that infrastructure under-theorised as a condition of political participation, carries no binding effect for the states that impose it, and states across Africa and beyond have learned to suppress it, with consequences that run past election day into the everyday accountability citizens exercise over public services. In this talk, we explore how human rights accountability frameworks must be redesigned to capture, measure, and remedy state-directed digital suppression as a violation of the right to political participation, and what Africa&amp;#39;s electoral experience contributes to building them.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Light lunch will be served.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://ircpl.columbia.edu" target="_blank"&gt;Institute for Religion, Culture and Public Life&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Questioning &amp;quot;World Religions&amp;quot;&lt;br /&gt;
A Conversation&lt;br /&gt;
Monday, November 16, 2026, 12:30-2:00pm&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Light lunch provided.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;From its invention in the late nineteenth century though to the present, the category of &amp;ldquo;world religion&amp;rdquo; has served not only to demarcate the boundaries of certain forms of interfaith dialogue, but also shaped how courts define and protect religious freedom, how scholars of religious studies imagine their fields of inquiry, and, more generally, how non specialists conceptualize what religion is and what all religions seem to share. This event will feature scholars in conversation about the category of &amp;ldquo;world religion&amp;rdquo; and the modern emergence of interfaith dialogue, touching on the history and legacy of Chicago&amp;rsquo;s &amp;ldquo;Parliament of World Religions&amp;rdquo; and other key moments in the institutionalization of interreligious relations in the United States and elsewhere.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Speakers: &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
Rebekah Coffman (Chicago History Museum)&lt;br /&gt;
Alexander Rocklin (Kenyon College)&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
